## the-pay-stone | attachment
!existing
> north the-boundary-cross

# ---- UNDER THE DEEP ROOTS ----------------------------------------------------

## the-soft-floor | attachment: the moss goes down into a hollow
!existing
> south the-sunk-moss

## the-sunk-moss | The Sunk Moss
The soft floor goes down here into a long hollow, the moss deeper still, so you wade in it to the shin, and it closes over each step behind you. Along one side of the hollow the ground rises into a bank, and roots come out of the bank in a wall.
> north the-soft-floor
> east the-under-bank

## the-under-bank | The Under-Bank
You go along under a bank where the ground has fallen away from the roots of a row of great trees, the roots hanging over you, tangled and dripping, furred with moss. Water drips from them onto your shoulders. Between them there are gaps, and behind the gaps it is dark.
> west the-sunk-moss
> east the-dark-behind

## the-dark-behind | The Dark Behind
Behind the wall of roots there is a space, a hollow under the trees where the ground has gone, roofed by roots and walled by roots, black and cool and smelling of wet earth. Something has slept here. Through the roots on the north side there is a grey of open wood.
> west the-under-bank
> north the-deep-roots

# ---- THE OLD GROWTH'S NORTH WAY ----------------------------------------------

## the-cut-line | The Cut Line
The coppice round the counting house stops here at a line as straight as a ruled edge, the stools on one side cut and cut again for a hundred years and the old trees on the other never cut at all. A ride runs along the line, kept open by feet. It goes east toward the old gatehouse ground.
> east the-tally-posts

## the-tally-posts | The Tally Posts
Oak posts stand along the ride at every twentieth pace, grey and split, each one notched down its side in fives the way the rods in the counting house are tied. Somebody walked this line and counted. The posts go on east to where the ground opens by the road.
> west the-cut-line
> east the-mounting-block

## the-mounting-block | attachment
!existing
> west the-tally-posts

# ---- THE GATEHOUSE ROAD ------------------------------------------------------

## the-rampart-walk | attachment: a gap in the back of the bank
!existing
> east the-postern-gap

## the-postern-gap | The Postern Gap
The bank of the old gatehouse ground is broken through here at its back, where a small gate once let people out without opening the great one. The gateposts are stumps. Beyond the gap a road goes off away from the gatehouse, down toward the wood.
> west the-rampart-walk
> south the-back-road

## the-back-road | The Back Road
A road runs off behind the gatehouse, metalled once with flint and still hard under the turf, wide enough for a cart. Nobody came to the front gate by this road. It went to the wood, for the wood's timber and charcoal, and it still goes there.
> north the-postern-gap
> east the-sunk-paving

## the-sunk-paving | The Sunk Paving
The road's paving has sunk in the middle under a long weight of carts, and the two ruts are worn into the stone a hand deep, and water lies in them, and moss along their lips. Grass has the crown of the road. The ruts go on east.
> west the-back-road
> east the-fallen-milestone

## the-fallen-milestone | The Fallen Milestone
A milestone lies on its face in the verge, its foot still in the hole it stood in. Somebody has dug round it and turned it partway, to read it, and let it fall back. Whatever it says is underneath.
> west the-sunk-paving
> east the-road-bend

## the-road-bend | The Road Bend
The road turns south here and starts down off the high ground toward the trees, the verges closing in, the first oaks leaning over it. Looking back, the bank of the gatehouse ground is a long green line against the sky.
> west the-fallen-milestone
> south the-holloway-head

## the-holloway-head | The Holloway Head
The road begins to sink between banks, worn down below the ground by the wheels and the rain, the banks rising on both sides to your knee, then your waist. Roots come out of them. The light from above narrows to a strip.
> north the-road-bend
> south the-deep-holloway

## the-deep-holloway | The Deep Holloway
The road runs at the bottom of a cut deeper than a man is tall, the banks sheer and overhung, the trees along their tops meeting over the road so it goes through a green tunnel. Your steps sound on the hard floor. Anything on the banks above could watch you all the way.
> north the-holloway-head
> south the-root-bridge

## the-root-bridge | The Root Bridge
The roots of two oaks on opposite banks have met over the holloway and grown into each other, so a bridge of living root crosses the road at the height of your head. Something uses it. The bark on top of it is worn smooth and the moss is gone.
> north the-deep-holloway
> south the-rotted-cart

## the-rotted-cart | The Rotted Cart
A cart stands in the holloway where it stopped, its wheels sunk to the hubs in the road's mud, its bed rotted through, the shafts lying on the ground in front of it with no harness. A load of charcoal is still in it, black under the leaf litter. It was going up the road, to the gatehouse.
> north the-root-bridge
> south the-washout

## the-washout | The Washout
Water has used the holloway as a stream for years and torn the road out of its bottom, leaving the old paving in broken slabs tumbled among stones. You climb down over them. The banks have slipped in places and the road is half buried.
> north the-rotted-cart
> south the-wet-lane

## the-wet-lane | The Wet Lane
The holloway shallows out and the road goes on as a wet lane between low banks, puddled from side to side, reeds coming up in the ruts. From ahead comes a great racket of frogs. On the bank to the west the ground drops away into a dark under roots.
> north the-washout
> south the-lanes-end

## the-lanes-end | The Lane's End
The road gives out here among reeds and standing water, the last of the paving going down under the wet and not coming up. It was a road to the wood and it reached it. The frogs are very loud.
> north the-wet-lane
> south the-frog-chorus

## the-frog-chorus | attachment
!existing
> north the-lanes-end
